timestamp=2026-11-04T16:00:04.064Z level=INFO run=e9a56b16 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# Now check rule 6 more carefully: \"If only one type of separator exists, treat it as a decimal separator only when exactly two digits follow its last occurrence.\"\n# For \"808.824\" - dot is the separator. After last occurrence (pos of last dot), there are 3 digits \"824\" - so after == 3, not 2. That means treat as NOT decimal separator. So what is it? Thousands separator? In many cases, 808.824 with 3 digits after might mean 808824 (no decimal) or 808 and 824 decimals depending on locale. But if we treat dot as thousands separator (not decimal), we remove dots -> 808824. That gives 808824. Is that the correct numeric interpretation? Or is it 808.824 as a decimal (meaning 808 + 0.824)?\n# Similarly 107.908 -> 107908 if not decimal. That looks like a large integer? Let us look at these rows in context.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
