timestamp=2026-11-04T16:00:38.370Z level=INFO run=e9a56b16 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# Also \"1.000\" - after dot 3 digits, not 2. So not decimal -> remove dots -> 1000. That matches. Also \"1.000,00\" has both; last separator is comma (pos of comma > dot), comma is last -> comma is decimal. And \"00\" after comma gives 2 digits - so comma as decimal is correct, and dots as thousands separators are removed -> gives 1000.00. Correct. That matches. Also \"1,000.00\" - dot is last, 2 digits after dot -> dot is decimal. Commas removed as thousands -> 1000.00. Correct. Good.\n# \"107.908\" - 3 digits after dot, so dot not decimal. Remove dots -> 107908. Is this right? Could it be 107.908 (107 + 0.908)? Or is it 107908? Both are possible. Let us look for similar patterns or maybe the expected scale. Also compare to other amounts - most amounts are 2-3 digits like 80.87, 105.16 etc. 808824 is a very large number. Also fees are like -18.08, small numbers. Maybe the dot in these cases is a thousands separator? Or maybe it's a decimal but with 3 digits (like milliseconds/cents in some formats)? But rule is explicit. We must follow the rule as written. So we implement exactly as specified.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
